WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.710
TODD GAGE: Good morning.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.940
My name's Todd Gage.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.750
I'm the Product Manager for
Persistent Disk and Local SSD.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.520
I want to thank you all for
joining us here this morning,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.480
especially at 9:00 AM after
a big party last night.

00:00:23.384 --> 00:00:25.300
So here's what we're
here to talk about today.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.080
We're going to talk a bit about
persistent disk, some best

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.949
practices, a little bit
about our future performance,

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:33.240
and then cover a whole bunch
of new features, which I'm

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.531
very excited to show you today.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.780
So for those of
you who have been

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.690
going to any of the
storage sessions thus far,

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.380
you've probably seen this slide.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.440
It gets a bit more
complicated every year,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.600
a bit more like an
eye chart every year.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.320
And so to help you out,
here's where we are.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.590
We're looking in the storage
section under block storage,

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.310
and we're going to be talking
about persistent disk.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.170
And Google Cloud Platform has
three block storage products,

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.690
local SSD, which is
physically attached,

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.180
physically resident
in the machine.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.650
This is ephemeral storage.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.020
If that machine goes
down, we don't guarantee

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.352
that that data remains.

00:01:12.352 --> 00:01:14.560
However, there's also the
highest performance storage

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.160
we have because it is physically
resonant in the machine.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.540
We have two flavors
of persistent disk.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.440
Now these are designed to be
durable persistent storage.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.080
And the two flavors, we have one
backed by standard hard drives,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.010
and that's really for cost
effective, high volume storage,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.085
and then one backed
by SSD, which

00:01:32.085 --> 00:01:34.990
is for your high
performance storage needs.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.810
I'm going to quickly talk
about just sort of overall PD.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.750
Now, as I said, PD is a high
performance durable block

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.190
storage for any of
your virtual instances,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.700
either on Google Kubernetes
Engine or Google Compute

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.690
Engine.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:52.240
Presents itself as a
virtual SCSI device.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.505
And it scales from one
gigabyte all the way up to 64T.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.330
Persistent disk supports multi
reader in read only mode, which

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.180
we'll talk about
a bit in a moment;

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.509
snapshots, which, again,
we'll cover in a moment;

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:13.740
and scale, again,
going from one gig

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.910
to 64T with the ability to
grow while that disk is in use.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.460
And as you've probably
heard throughout this week,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.340
security is very, very
key component Google Cloud

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.210
Platform.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.700
And so all of our data
is encrypted at rest

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:33.820
automatically either via Google
encryption keys or your own.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.760
We see a couple common
patterns of behavior

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.210
with persistent disk.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.300
This is the simplest.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.877
This could be your basic
web server, WordPress,

00:02:41.877 --> 00:02:43.960
anything like that where
you just have one volume,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.720
one virtual machine.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.650
Another common pattern is a
root disk and then some data.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.470
This could be your
database instance, anything

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.820
where you have working data.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:58.271
Again, not very complicated.

00:02:58.271 --> 00:03:00.270
This is where things get
to be more interesting.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.040
This could be a big
compute workload

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.520
where you have one data disk,
you put it in read only mode.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.580
You shard out all
that work to workers.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.240
And you don't actually have
to multiply record that data.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.982
You just have all those
instances point at one disk.

00:03:16.982 --> 00:03:18.690
And then finally,
something, again, we'll

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.400
talk about a bit more
in a bit is the ability

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.850
to synchronously
replicate data across two

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.640
zones with a new offering
that we're calling Regional

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.460
Persistent Disk.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.120
So persistent disk snapshots
I mentioned a second ago.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.000
Persistent disk snapshots are
used for a number of things.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.570
One is just basic
backup of your data.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.380
This data is critical
to your business.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.963
You want to make
sure that you have it

00:03:44.963 --> 00:03:46.670
around in case of future need.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.850
Snapshots are of
great use for that.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.180
Disaster recovery.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.480
You want to insulate your
business against disaster.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.080
You can take snapshots
that are stored in a GCS

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.550
multi-regional bucket,
and that ensures you

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:01.250
against the data
loss in any one zone.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:03.740
And then finally, disk cloning.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.040
Snapshots allow you to really
quickly replicate disks

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.860
across multiple
regions, multiple zones.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:14.900
And the first disk is relatively
fast, and every disk after that

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:15.980
will be very, very fast.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:22.220
Here's a couple examples
of snapshots in use.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.500
On the left, we have
Volume 1 and you

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.560
see we are taking
snapshots at T1 through T4.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.570
Each of those snapshots
is a point in time,

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.490
but it's also incremental,
so you're only

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.560
storing the additional
data that you need

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.760
from the previous snapshot.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.920
Now on the right,
you see this user

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.250
is taking a copy of
Volume 1 at Time 2,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.370
and then also doing disk
cloning at the bottom,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.210
making three copies
of Volume 1 at Time 4,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.595
potentially for use across
three different machines.

00:04:59.774 --> 00:05:01.940
Couple of best practices
to go through this morning.

00:05:04.974 --> 00:05:06.390
One thing I didn't
mention earlier

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.980
but you might have
read on the slide

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.980
is that persistent disk
gives you maximum performance

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.170
without the need
for RAID or striping

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:14.990
across multiple volumes.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.280
Persistent disk
performance is exclusively

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.160
tied to the disk
size, so we give you

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.720
an allocation of I/O per
gigabyte and bandwidth

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.660
per gigabyte that increases
linearly up to the bandwidth

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.350
caps, or up to the caps.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:34.640
So unlike other platforms
you may have seen,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.370
there's no additional need to
add volumes for performance.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:44.150
Also, we really suggest
you look at both your I/O

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.070
size and queue depths.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:50.630
So for high IOPS workloads, we
suggest 16 kilobyte block sizes

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.470
or smaller, and larger
I/O queue depths,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:58.430
which really help performance
at those lower block sizes.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:03.110
Specifically, for high I/O,
we suggest a queue depth of 1

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.170
for every 400-800 IOPS.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.470
Now on the other side, on
high throughput workloads,

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:14.040
we suggest block sizes
of 256K or larger

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.190
and enabling the read
ahead cache in the OS.

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.980
Finally, we really
suggest aligning your I/O

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.690
to 4K boundaries.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:28.630
What you see on the
right is an example of 6K

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.039
writes at the top, how
persistent disk sees this

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:34.080
in the middle, and then
how it's actually written

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.060
to the volume on the bottom.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.710
And what's a little
less apparent

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:39.920
is that for every 6K
write, you actually

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.160
have to touch two blocks.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.050
So you're effectively
doing 30% more disk access

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.440
for the same amount of
traffic, where if you just

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.320
aligned your rights
to four 4K boundaries,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.000
you can save those
extra disk accesses.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:03.440
I'm now very excited to share
with you some announcements,

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.094
starting with performance.

00:07:05.094 --> 00:07:07.010
Performance is the thing
that we hear the most

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:08.051
about from our customers.

00:07:08.051 --> 00:07:10.820
It's consistent in literally
every customer conversation,

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.790
and it's something that we're
always focused on improving.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.486
As I said earlier, persistence
disk comes in two flavors.

00:07:18.486 --> 00:07:20.110
We have standard
persistent disk backed

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.620
by HDD and SSD persistent disk.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.320
And then you can really see the
stark difference in performance

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.370
here.

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.340
SSD doubles the IOPS
on the right side

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:35.630
and is more than an order of
magnitude faster on reads.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.790
One thing to note, as I
say in the corner here,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:41.540
this does require machines
with 32 vCPUs or larger

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:46.290
on the SSD side to
achieve these IOPS.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:52.380
And now I'm very, very excited
to announce a 50% improvement

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.980
in read performance for PD SSD.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.240
This increases our
read IOPS to 60,000

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.110
and our bandwidth
to 1.2 gigabytes.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.770
Now some lucky few of
you may have already seen

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.000
this performance in production.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.670
But if you haven't, don't worry.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.785
It will be rolling
out to all of PD SSD

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:17.160
over the next couple of weeks,
and I'm very, very excited

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.640
to see people take
advantage of it.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.180
Now, as I said, we're always
working on performance,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.810
and we're not stopping there.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:27.780
So we do have something
in our performance labs

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.160
that we're working on.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.280
And this is a number of changes
kind of baked in together.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.789
But as you can see, we're
getting all the way up

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:39.929
to 100K IOPS and 1.6
gigabytes of bandwidth.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:41.400
And the most
exciting thing for us

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.132
is how that translates into
real world performance.

00:08:45.132 --> 00:08:46.590
On the right-hand
side, you can see

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:49.740
we did some read-only
benchmarking against Postgres

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:53.220
and Aerospike and saw
almost a 30% improvement

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:57.265
on Postgres and over a 40%
improvement on Aerospike.

00:08:57.265 --> 00:08:58.890
Now, as I mentioned,
in the lab there's

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.480
a couple of different
changes going on.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:03.150
But as we dug into
this, it looks

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.600
like that performance change is
primarily driven by the IOPS.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.660
And we're really
excited to get this out.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.250
No promises yet,
but as I said, it's

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.200
something we're
actively working on.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.007
Now, earlier I mentioned
regional persistent disk,

00:09:20.007 --> 00:09:22.590
and now I'd like to talk about
it in a little bit more detail.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.620
So regional persistent
disk presents itself

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.990
as a singular persistent
disk to your virtual machine.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.200
However, on the back
end, it is synchronously

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.440
replicated across two zones.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.390
Regional persistent
disk works either

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.330
with Google Kubernetes Engine
or Google Compute Engine

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:49.260
and allows you a single
disk for high availability.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.540
One thing to note,
regional persistent disk

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:53.910
has all the performance
of a persistent disk

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.190
other than right bandwidth.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.320
Right bandwidth is
halved because you're

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.890
writing the data to two places,
but otherwise the performance

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:02.800
is exactly the same.

00:10:05.574 --> 00:10:06.990
So regional
persistent disk really

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.790
provides you a building
block for building

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.990
either disaster recovery
solutions or high availability

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.920
solutions, depending on what
you need for your business.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.230
It improves durability
and availability.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.990
And we've really spent time
building this for mission

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.530
critical applications.

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:26.670
In fact, for any of you
that are using Cloud SQL's

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:28.740
high availability
configuration, that

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.100
is backed by regional
persistent disk,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.720
and it has been a very
successful product for us.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:38.350
This, as I note, is in beta
and it's available today.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:42.690
You can just go to your
instance, open a new disk,

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:47.380
and you will see
something like this.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.830
So this is the new UI.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:54.900
You can replicate this
disk within a region.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.060
That changes the zone dropdown.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.240
And instead of zone
it's now zones,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.950
and you're able to select up
to two zones in that region.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:06.130
So it's very, very
straightforward to set up,

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.860
almost as easy as setting
up a normal persistent disk,

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:11.410
but automatically
gets you replication

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:12.500
across these two zones.

00:11:15.689 --> 00:11:17.230
And now to show you
a little bit more

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.490
about regional persistent
disk in practice,

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.190
I'd like to introduce Saad Ali.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:26.597
He's the tech lead for Google
Kubernetes Engine Storage.

00:11:26.597 --> 00:11:27.430
SAAD ALI: All right.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.780
Thank you very much, Todd.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:30.850
So my name is Saad Ali.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:34.270
I'm tech lead of GKE storage,
and I'm really, really excited

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:38.020
about this regional
persistent disk feature.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.250
Let me talk a little
bit about it today.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:43.210
So like Todd mentioned,
regional persistent disks

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.650
are a great building block
for building HA applications.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:50.920
It'll automatically handle
any storage level failures.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.950
It'll automatically
failover with lower latency.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:58.220
But it'll be invisible
to you as an end user.

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:01.150
But what happens if you
have VM level failure,

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:05.240
you have zonal failures and
the VMs become unavailable?

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.880
Normally you'd have an outage
until somebody on your team

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:11.390
notices and goes in and manually
moves that workload over.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.630
It's nice that you have the
replication across zones,

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.910
but you still require
manual intervention in order

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.520
to make your application HA.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.280
Or you could build
custom tooling

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:23.900
that will go ahead and
monitor the virtual machines,

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.030
monitor your application and
do the failover manually.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.550
But that's a lot of
complicated tooling.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.880
Wouldn't it be nice if there was
something that did this for you

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.010
Automatically

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:38.900
Kubernetes Engine.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.340
Google Kubernetes Engine handles
all of this for you already.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.720
By a show of hands,
how many of you

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.190
are familiar with Kubernetes?

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.480
That's what I like to see.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.750
And how many of you use GKE?

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:52.890
Awesome.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:57.060
So with GKE, we do node level
health monitoring already

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:57.880
for you.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:01.540
And we automatically
will move your workloads

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.090
if we detect that the
node is unhealthy.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.870
And along with your workloads,
we'll move your storage.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.697
You don't have to do a thing.

00:13:09.697 --> 00:13:11.530
So the next question
is, how do you actually

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.372
use this in Kubernetes?

00:13:13.372 --> 00:13:14.830
If you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:18.410
you've probably already seen
the storage class API object.

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:20.440
It enables Kubernetes
cluster administrators

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.010
to specify a template for
provisioning new volumes.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:26.950
So when you as an end
user want to use storage,

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.730
you specify a storage class in
your persistent volume object,

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.410
and a new volume
gets provisioned

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.890
using that template.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:36.990
So to enable
automatic provisioning

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:39.600
of regional persistent
disks, all you have to do

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.010
is add a couple
of new parameters

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.630
to your storage class.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.850
One is the replication
type to indicate

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.280
that you want a regional
persistent disk,

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.050
and the second is
zones to indicate

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:51.510
which zones you want your
persistent disk to be

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.410
replicated in.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.050
And then Kubernetes
will automatically

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.810
provision a new regional
persistent disk for you,

00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:59.700
and you'll use it in
exactly the same way

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.570
you're used to using persistent
volumes in Kubernetes today.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:10.050
So now let's take a look at how
it's going to work in practice.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:13.790
So imagine you have two zones,
and each has a single zone.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:18.050
And you have a workload that's
running on the node in Zone 1,

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:26.000
and a replicated disk is
attached to that workload.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.280
But now Zone 1 suffers
some sort of failure

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:32.210
and the node in Zone 1
is no longer available.

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:35.150
Kubernetes is automatically
going to detect this,

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:38.300
and it's going to terminate
your app from the unhealthy zone

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:40.190
and prepare it for moving.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.230
And it'll automatically
detach the volume

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:45.170
from the node in the
first zone and attach it

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:47.840
to the healthy node
in the second zone,

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:49.820
and move your
workload over as well.

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:51.800
And your stateful
service basically

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.350
automatically heals
from a zonal failure

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.584
without you having
to intervene at all.

00:14:56.584 --> 00:14:58.250
You didn't have to
write custom tooling.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.680
You didn't have to do
any manual intervention.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:03.800
That's the beauty of
regional persistent disks.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:07.160
So regional persistent disks
are available as a beta feature

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.567
starting with Kubernetes 110.

00:15:09.567 --> 00:15:11.150
Please try it out
and let us know what

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.451
you think Thank you very much.

00:15:13.451 --> 00:15:14.200
Back to you, Todd.

00:15:16.397 --> 00:15:17.522
TODD GAGE: Thank you, Saad.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:25.379
So I realize I didn't
cover one key thing

00:15:25.379 --> 00:15:26.920
about regional
persistent disk, which

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.130
is it is available in both
flavors of persistent disk.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:31.870
You can do regional
persistent disk

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:35.950
or standard persistent disk or
SSD persistent disk depending

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.530
on your business need.

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:43.030
I now want to talk about
regional snapshots.

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:46.030
Regional snapshots allow
users, for the first time,

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.390
to specify the locations
that their snapshots land in.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:51.940
As I mentioned earlier today,
snapshots automatically

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.180
get recorded in a GCS
multi-regional bucket.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.760
And going forward, users will
be able to select whether they

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:00.370
wish a region or
multi-regional bucket,

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:02.830
and then can select any
region or multi-region

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:04.690
within the Google network.

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:06.430
This really provides
users a choice

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.700
between whether they want
insulation against disaster

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:13.630
recovery or data
residency, where you really

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:16.210
need the data to stay
in a single location

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:18.860
for legal or regulatory reasons.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:21.160
This also, for the
first time, means

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.080
that snapshots
are going to begin

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.120
to charge for network transit.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:29.000
And so there's also a
cost effective play here,

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.420
where if you want to
avoid network transit,

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:35.194
you can keep those snapshots
exactly where the disk is,

00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:36.610
and all you pay
for is the storage

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:38.570
you're paying for today.

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:40.610
If you have any interest
in this whatsoever,

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:43.949
please email
pdinterest@google.com

00:16:43.949 --> 00:16:45.490
and I'll be very,
very happy to share

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:48.810
with you more information.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:52.400
Here's an example of what
this looks like in practice.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.724
I'm going to highlight the
key features for you here.

00:16:54.724 --> 00:16:56.390
On the left-hand side,
we see that we're

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:59.060
setting up a regional snapshot.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:01.430
And I've selected
the default, which

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:04.849
is just have the snapshot reside
in the exact same location

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:05.900
as the disk.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.760
As I said earlier,
this is a setup

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:11.210
we see fairly commonly for
data residency, data locality

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:15.470
concerns, and/or to
drive costs down.

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:17.420
On the right, you
can see I'm selecting

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:21.290
a multi-regional bucket and
moving the data to the EU.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.839
And this would be, again,
for disaster recovery.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:26.150
Or, if I know that
I need to replicate

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:29.720
that disk multiple times
in the EU in the future,

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:33.470
I can write that data to the EU
once and then replicate locally

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:36.810
many, many times, again, for
cost savings on the network

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:37.310
transit.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.180
Your snapshot list will
also begin to change.

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:46.762
And there is a new
column here for location.

00:17:46.762 --> 00:17:47.970
It's the second column there.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.310
It just shows you where
these snapshots reside,

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:54.630
or where the people need
snapshot management tools.

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:58.770
And this will really help you
find all of your snapshots

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.100
based on location
using the filter.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:06.830
Another exciting
feature for snapshots

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:11.770
I'm happy to talk about
is snapshot scheduling.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:14.620
Snapshot scheduling provides
lifecycle management

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:18.640
for snapshots from
creation to deletion.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:22.910
You can schedule per
hour, by day, or by week.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.180
It supports snapshot locality.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:27.400
And again, if this is
something that's of interest,

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.530
please email
pdinterest@google.com.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.810
And here's an example of
setting up a daily schedule.

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:38.900
And you can see selected
a regional bucket.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:42.410
But then, down in the
frequency, selected every day

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:43.770
at 3 o'clock.

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:48.029
So this snapshot will kick
off in that hour once a day.

00:18:48.029 --> 00:18:50.570
And then all of those snapshots
will be deleted automatically

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:51.650
14 days later.

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.940
In this instance, I'm
setting up a weekly schedule.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:00.740
And as you see, I'm specifying
the day of the week,

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:05.991
and then which hour of that day
I want the snapshot created.

00:19:05.991 --> 00:19:08.240
Now, what you can't see is
on the bottom of the screen

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:09.850
there's still the
auto delete row.

00:19:09.850 --> 00:19:11.930
It just is off the screen.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:14.600
But every snapshot schedule
includes an audio deletion

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:16.424
policy.

00:19:16.424 --> 00:19:18.590
I'm not going to show you
the hourly schedule today,

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:22.144
but it's very much in
line with these two.

00:19:22.144 --> 00:19:23.810
However, what I will
show you is how you

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.452
attach a schedule to a disk.

00:19:26.452 --> 00:19:28.160
Now, at the top when
you select the disk,

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:32.110
every disk today can only
have one attached schedule.

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:36.260
However, you can use a
schedule for many, many disks.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.350
So if you know that you
have some data that you want

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.270
to back up every
day, some data you

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:43.730
want to back up every week--

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:47.940
don't have monthly yet,
but it's in the works--

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:52.580
or every hour, then you can set
up those schedules singularly,

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:54.890
and then attach that
to all the data disks

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:56.450
that it applies to
so you don't have

00:19:56.450 --> 00:19:57.920
to manage many, many schedules.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.530
So again, if you have any
questions about anything

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:10.170
that you've seen today, please
email pdinterest@google.com.

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:14.700
That's for alpha signups,
questions about best practices,

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:17.600
or any other things
you would like to know.

